From f3a180df4bd3dce538997985bdfc4cfc493b2b8a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: unex <7575866+unex@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 11 Oct 2026 14:02:20 -0600
Subject: [PATCH] control: player-vol, player-mute and --volume-file

The control socket could only ask the server for the *group* volume
and mute. A device that embeds this player and has its own volume
control (a panel slider, a knob) had no way to set this endpoint's own
output level, and no way to show a level the server changed.

Add two locally answered subcommands:

  player-vol <0-100>   apply this endpoint's own volume
  player-mute on|off   apply this endpoint's own mute

Like a server's player command, they set the sink, persist the pair in
the state store, and go out to the server in client/state through
PlayerRole::update_volume() / update_muted(), so the server and every
controller show the new figure. They are never refused: a disconnected
player applies them and reports them at the next handshake. `status`
marks such a volume "(set on this host)".

Add --volume-file <path> (config key volume-file): the player writes
its applied volume and mute there at start and on every change, from a
server or from player-vol / player-mute, in the state file's
`key = value` format, mode 0644, replaced atomically. A local display
can follow the volume without access to the 0600 control socket and
without polling `status`. The atomic write is shared with the state
store.
