-Twelve of those go out to the server as `controller@v1` commands. Two never leave the host:
-`status`, formatted from the daemon's own view, and `delay`, which drives this endpoint's
-own player role.
+Twelve of those go out to the server as `controller@v1` commands. Four never leave the host
+as commands: `status`, formatted from the daemon's own view, and `delay`, `player-vol` and
+`player-mute`, which drive this endpoint's own player role.
+
+## The player's own volume
+
+`vol` and `mute` ask the server to change the group. `player-vol` and `player-mute` change
+only this box, the way a volume knob on the device would: the gain is applied at once,
+remembered across restarts like a server-set volume, and the player reports it to the server
+in its `client/state`, so the server and every controller show the new figure. With no server
+connected the change still applies, and the next handshake reports it. `status` then says
+`player volume: 40 (set on this host)` until a server sets it again.
+
+```console
+$ sendspin-cli player-vol 40
+$ sendspin-cli player-mute on
+```
+
+**`--volume-file <path>`** is the other direction: the player writes its applied volume and
+mute to `<path>` at start and on every change, whoever made it — a server, an announcement
+that raises and then restores the volume, or `player-vol`. It is the state file's format, at
+mode `0644` so a local display or panel can read it without access to the control socket,
+replaced atomically, so a reader never sees half a file:
+
+```text
+# Written by sendspin-cli. Edits are overwritten.
+volume = 40
+muted = false
+```
+
+Put it on a tmpfs (`/run`), since it changes as often as the volume does.
 
 ## Three that are easy to misread
 
